prompt:
Add `spawn chunks gc [--grace <minutes>] [--root <dir>] [--repo <path>]`. It removes chunk-store objects that no reachable commit names. Read docs/storage.md (the chunk store, workspace snapshots) and crates/spawn-host/src/chunks.rs first.

The chunk store only grows: every checkpoint's chunks and manifests stay forever, including those of dropped branches and of checkpoints that never got a commit. Required behaviour:

1. Where to look. The store is SPAWN_CHUNK_STORE, else <root>/.chunks, with root from --root, else SPAWN_WORKSPACE_ROOT. The repositories are the default repository (--repo, else SPAWN_GIT_REPO) and every bare repository directly under <root>/.sources/ (the mission mirrors).
2. What is live. Walk every commit reachable from any ref of any of those repositories, not only branch heads: an older commit of a branch still restores its files. Each `Spawn-Large: <name>` trailer names a manifest. The manifest and every chunk it lists are live.
3. What goes. An object (a file named by 64 hex digits under <store>/<2 hex>/) is removed only when it is not live and its modification time is older than the grace period (--grace, in minutes, default 60). Check the time again just before removing the object.
4. A put of an object the store already holds makes it young again: it refreshes the object's modification time. Chunks are written before the commit that names them, so a checkpoint in flight that reuses an old, unreferenced chunk must not lose it to a collection. A checkpoint that takes chunk names from its stat cache (<root>/.large/) must not name a chunk the store no longer holds. Check that it is there, and chunk the file again if it is not.
5. Read errors delete nothing. Decide everything before deleting anything. Stop with a non-zero exit and delete nothing when a repository cannot be read (the message names the repository's path), or when a manifest that a reachable commit names is missing, unreadable or does not verify (the message names the manifest by its 64-hex name). Such a manifest is never treated as unreferenced.
6. A second collection with nothing new removes nothing. Print a one-line summary: objects kept, objects removed, bytes freed.

Put listing and removal behind the chunk-store port (each object with its age), so another adapter (an S3-compatible bucket) can be collected the same way. Document the command, the roots and the grace rule in docs/storage.md. Add unit tests: live set from trailers and manifests, the grace boundary, the refresh on put, and refusal on a missing or corrupt manifest.

The gate judges this with rsi/scenarios/005.sh (read it). Real Bridges with a fake engine write 10 MiB files through the store. Agent one writes a file and then edits it in place, so an older commit of its branch names the file before the edit. A mission agent's branch lives only in the mission mirror under <root>/.sources/. Other agents write files and then their branches are dropped (`git update-ref -d`); objects are aged with `touch`. Expected with `spawn chunks gc --grace 30`:
- fresh unreferenced objects survive;
- old unreferenced objects go;
- the objects of an agent that rewrites the same bytes into old, unreferenced objects survive, because the put refreshed them, until they are old again;
- everything any reachable commit names survives every collection and still materializes (a fork from the older commit, resumes of both agents);
- an unreadable mirror (mode 000) means a non-zero exit, nothing deleted, and the mirror named;
- a second collection changes nothing;
- a corrupted manifest named by a branch head means a non-zero exit, nothing deleted, and the manifest named.

The scenario exits 0 when every check passes, 1 when an assertion fails, 2 on a harness or infrastructure failure.

Rules: never edit rsi/, scripts/rsi/, scripts/blackbox/ or scripts/e2e.sh (the gate rejects any change there). The gate also rejects fewer runnable tests than the base (do not delete, ignore or disable tests), any lint relaxation ([lints] tables, clippy.toml, new crate-level #![allow]/#![expect], a removed #![warn]/#![deny]), and any build-time code (build.rs, .cargo/, a `build =` key in a Cargo.toml). The gate builds offline from the cargo caches already on the host: a new crate builds only if it is already in ~/.cargo/registry. All existing checks must still pass: clippy, the workspace tests, the e2e, the black-box scenarios and the web build.
